<section class="section section-with-bar bar-section animate" aria-labelledby="not-title">
  <span class="title-tag bar-eyebrow" aria-hidden="true">What it does not do</span>
  <h2 class="section-title" id="not-title">What it never touches</h2>
  <p class="showcase-subtitle">The list that matters more than the feature list.</p>

  <div class="container">
    <div class="glass-info-card-grid">
      <div class="glass-info-card glass-card" style="--color:#22c55e"><span class="info-accent"></span>
        <h3>It does not modify your games</h3>
        <p>No file in a game folder is written, patched or replaced. The app reads Fortnite's install path for exactly one purpose: to show the game's own icon in the interface.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="glass-info-card glass-card" style="--color:#22c55e"><span class="info-accent"></span>
        <h3>It does not inject into processes</h3>
        <p>There is no code anywhere in the app that writes into another program's memory or hooks into a running game. Nothing attaches to your game while you play.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="glass-info-card glass-card" style="--color:#22c55e"><span class="info-accent"></span>
        <h3>It does not bundle third-party tools</h3>
        <p>No drivers, no kernel components, no repackaged utilities from somewhere else. It uses Windows' own interfaces to change Windows' own settings.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="glass-info-card glass-card" style="--color:#22c55e"><span class="info-accent"></span>
        <h3>It does not keep your tweaks on disk</h3>
        <p>Paid tweak definitions arrive from the server after you sign in and are held in memory for that session only.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section animate" aria-labelledby="undo-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header section-header--left">
      <span class="title-tag bar-eyebrow" aria-hidden="true">Undo</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" id="undo-title">Every change can be reversed</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="legal-layout legal-layout--single">
      <div class="legal-prose">
        <p>Three ways out, in increasing order of bluntness:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Revert a single tweak.</b> Every applied tweak has a revert next to it.</li>
          <li><b>Revert a whole category.</b> Undo all 43 network tweaks at once if your ping got worse instead of better.</li>
          <li><b>Roll back the machine.</b> The app can create a Windows restore point before it changes anything, from any category header or the Restore points tool.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The NVIDIA graphics profile is undone differently and deliberately so: instead of
          reversing each value, it imports NVIDIA's own stock Fortnite profile. A half-finished
          revert cannot leave your driver in a state nobody designed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section animate" aria-labelledby="anticheat-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header section-header--left">
      <span class="title-tag bar-eyebrow" aria-hidden="true">Anti-cheat</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" id="anticheat-title">What we will not promise you</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="legal-layout legal-layout--single">
      <div class="legal-prose">
        <p>
          Plenty of sites in this category will tell you their software is guaranteed safe with
          every anti-cheat. We are not going to, because nobody outside Riot, Epic or Valve can
          promise that about software they do not control.
        </p>
        <p>What we can tell you precisely:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>The tweaks change Windows, not your game.</b> Nothing is written into a game
            folder and nothing attaches to a running process, which is what anti-cheat systems
            are built to detect.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>The crosshair overlay is the one thing to check first.</b> It draws on top of your
            game, and some anti-cheat software disallows overlays. Check your game's rules before
            using it competitively. The app tells you the same thing on the crosshair screen.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If you play something with a strict overlay policy, use everything else and leave the
          crosshair off. Nothing else in the app puts anything on your screen while you play.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-with-bar bar-section faq-section animate" aria-labelledby="safe-faq-title">
