    <span class="section-label">Support</span>
    <h2 class="faq-title" id="safe-faq-title">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <p class="faq-subtitle">Safety, bans, reversibility and what happens if something goes wrong</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-list" data-accordion>
    <div class="faq-item"><h3><button type="button" class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Will MizTweakz get me banned?<span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"><i data-icon="plus"></i></span></button></h3><div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">The tweaks change Windows settings and never touch your game files or running processes, which is what anti-cheat systems look for. The one thing to check is the crosshair overlay, because some anti-cheat software disallows overlays. Check your game's rules before using it competitively, and leave it off if you are unsure.</div></div></div>
    <div class="faq-item"><h3><button type="button" class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">What if a tweak makes something stop working?<span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"><i data-icon="plus"></i></span></button></h3><div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Revert it. Every applied tweak can be undone individually, a whole category can be reverted at once, and the app can create a Windows restore point before it changes anything so you can roll the machine back entirely.</div></div></div>
    <div class="faq-item"><h3><button type="button" class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Why does it need administrator rights?<span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"><i data-icon="plus"></i></span></button></h3><div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Most tweaks write to the registry, Windows services and scheduled tasks, and Windows requires administrator rights for all three. The installer asks once and the app asks each time it launches.</div></div></div>
    <div class="faq-item"><h3><button type="button" class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Is every tweak safe to apply?<span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"><i data-icon="plus"></i></span></button></h3><div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">414 of the 525 are labelled safe and 111 are labelled caution, and the app shows you the label before you apply anything. Caution does not mean dangerous, it means the tweak turns off something you might actually use. Disabling Bluetooth services is sensible on a desktop and wrong on a laptop with wireless headphones.</div></div></div>
    <div class="faq-item"><h3><button type="button" class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Do PC tweaks actually do anything?<span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"><i data-icon="plus"></i></span></button></h3><div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Individually, mostly very little. The app rates 391 of the 525 as low impact, 108 as medium and 26 as high. The gain comes from applying a lot of them together, and it is larger on a machine carrying years of background software than on a clean install.</div></div></div>
    <div class="faq-item"><h3><button type="button" class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Does it work on Windows 10?<span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"><i data-icon="plus"></i></span></button></h3><div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Yes. Windows 10 and Windows 11, on desktops and laptops. Results vary with your hardware and with how much is already running in the background.</div></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-cta">
    <p class="cta-text">Still not sure?</p>
    <a class="cta-link" href="{{ settings.discord_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ask us on Discord <i data-icon="arrow-right"></i></a>
